<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute periapical abscess of tooth 19, drained and extracted (K04.7)</content>. The radiograph shows deep decay under a fractured crown with a periapical radiolucency; the tooth could not be restored and was extracted after drainage. The infection is localized, with no fever, trismus or fascial-space spread. With definitive treatment done, antibiotics would add harm without benefit, as the ADA 2019 guideline advises, even with her new diabetes, provided she is watched for spread. Dr. Lindqvist-Bello and I agree the plan. [S]<list><item>No antibiotic; chlorhexidine rinse twice daily for two weeks, starting this evening.</item><item>Ibuprofen with acetaminophen for a few more days; no opioid.</item><item>Soft food on the right; no smoking or straws.</item><item>Go to the emergency department at once for swelling spreading to the eye, floor of mouth or neck, difficulty swallowing or breathing, trouble opening the mouth, or fever.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Periodontitis, generalized, stage III, grade C (K05.323)</content>. The radiograph shows horizontal bone loss to the middle third of the roots, and two molars were lost earlier to gum disease; her A1c above 7% makes it grade C. Periodontitis and diabetes worsen each other, and treating the gums can lower A1c a little. [S]<list><item>Scaling and root planing of all teeth with the dental team once the socket has healed, then maintenance every three months.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Type 2 diabetes, new (E11.65)</content>. First dose of metformin tolerated. A1c 7.6% at diagnosis; the infection can raise glucose for a few days. [S]<list><item>Metformin 500 mg with the evening meal for a week, then 1000 mg.</item><item>Diabetes education and nutrition sessions booked.</item><item>Eye and foot screening at the six-week visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Dental radiograph of the infected tooth and periodontium</td><td>At presentation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Panoramic radiograph: periapical radiolucency at tooth 19; generalized bone loss to the middle third of the roots</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Definitive treatment of the abscess</td><td>Within 24 hours for a localized abscess</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Incision and drainage and extraction of tooth 19 this morning</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antibiotic stewardship</td><td>At each dental assessment</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Localized abscess with definitive treatment done and no systemic involvement; no antibiotic</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Dental pain relief without opioids</td><td>During acute dental pain</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Ibuprofen with acetaminophen; pain easing</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Spreading-infection warning signs</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None today; warning signs reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Periodontal staging and grading</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Stage III, grade C</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Metformin: dose, tolerance and sick-day rules</td><td>At start and every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>First dose tolerated; sick-day rules explained</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Diabetes self-management education and nutrition therapy</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Sessions booked to start next week</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Comprehensive foot examination</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Due</td><td>Planned for the six-week visit, once the dental infection has settled</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Diabetic eye screening</td><td>At diagnosis, then every one to two years</td><td>Due</td><td>Retinal photography planned for the six-week visit</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Oral and maxillofacial surgery consultation</td><td>Within 24 hours of intake</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Lindqvist-Bello this morning</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Dental infection drained and the tooth removed within 24 hours, with no spread of infection. Outcome by 2026-04-15: Drained and extracted the next day; no spread [S]</item><item>Hemoglobin A1c below 7% within four months. In progress; target 2026-07-21 [S]</item><item>LDL cholesterol below 100 mg/dL on a statin. In progress; target 2026-07-21 [S]</item><item>Periodontal therapy completed and three-monthly maintenance arranged. In progress; target 2026-07-21 [S]</item><item>Climb stairs at the building sites with less knee pain, and walk 150 minutes a week. In progress; target 2026-07-21 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Standards of Care in Diabetes—2026 (Section 2, Diagnosis and Classification of Diabetes; Section 9, Pharmacologic Approaches to Glycemic Treatment) (2026); Evidence-based clinical practice guideline on antibiotic use for the urgent management of pulpal- and periapical-related dental pain and intraoral swelling (2019); Evidence-based clinical practice guideline for the pharmacologic management of acute dental pain in adolescents, adults, and older adults (2024); Scientific evidence on the links between periodontal diseases and diabetes: Consensus report and guidelines of the joint workshop on periodontal diseases and diabetes by the International Diabetes Federation and the European Federation of Periodontology (2018); Staging and grading of periodontitis: Framework and proposal of a new classification and case definition (2018); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, diabetes type 2, rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis, and hyperlipidemia monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Six-week review for healing, diabetes, eye and foot screening, and cardiovascular risk. Emergency department at once for any sign of spreading infection. Next scheduled visit 2026-05-26: six-week review: healing, diabetes, cardiovascular risk, eye and foot screening, and the start of semaglutide. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
